# Shared

## Summary

What the modules share: the staff member who uses the admin, the organisation they work for, and the files attached to records. A staff member works for one organisation at a time, the one they signed in to, and records a module creates belong to that organisation. The file store that keeps the files is required by the admin itself (`juicebar`).

## Requirements

- **MEDIATYPES** — A file is uploaded under one of the media types the `media` configuration offers for the record's collection, and the type sets its key and its kind of file. A record whose collection has no media types offers none to choose.
- **MEDIASIZE** — A file larger than its media type's limit is refused; without a limit on the type, the media service's default of 9,437,000 bytes applies.
- **MEDIALIST** — A record's files are listed with their title and media type. Images and videos can be previewed and other files downloaded. A file's name, title, description, alternative text and media type can be edited, and removing a file asks for confirmation first.

## Tests

- Uploading an 11 MB image under a media type without its own limit is refused.
- Removing a file asks "Delete media" with the file's title before it is taken off the record.

## Persona: Staff

### Summary

A person with an account in the admin.

### Role

- Signs in to the admin for one of their organisations; what they may do there depends on the roles given to them

## Record: Organisation

### Summary

An organisation of the project: a company or a part of one, whose staff use the admin and whose records it keeps.

### Schema

```schema
id: string required unique
name: string
```

## Record: Media

### Summary

One file on a record (an image, a document or a video), as the media service stored it, with the details staff gave it. Texts are kept per language.

### Schema

```schema
id: string required
fileName: string required
mimeType: string required
fullPath: string required
thumbPath: string
key: string
type: string
mediaType: map(string)
name: map(string)
title: map(string)
description: map(string)
alt_text: map(string)
```

### Constraints

- `key` is the media type's key and `type` the kind of file within it; `mediaType` is the media type's title.
- Some older records keep a text detail as one text instead of one per language.
